WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.491
Kubernetes deployment scripts run with
identities.

2
00:00:05.571 --> 00:00:12.116
That identity may come from a kubeconfig
file, service account token,

3
00:00:12.196 --> 00:00:20.182
CI/CD runner, workload identity, platform
identity, or managed deployment system.

4
00:00:20.262 --> 00:00:28.248
Whatever the source, the script's
permissions are the permissions of that identity.

5
00:00:28.328 --> 00:00:33.985
If the identity is broad, the script has a
broad blast radius.

6
00:00:34.065 --> 00:00:41.718
kubeconfig files, tokens, and
service-account credentials should be treated as

7
00:00:41.798 --> 00:00:44.128
sensitive credentials.

8
00:00:44.208 --> 00:00:50.974
They should have owners, controlled
storage, environment boundaries,

9
00:00:51.054 --> 00:00:56.933
rotation expectations, revocation paths,
and access review.

10
00:00:57.013 --> 00:01:04.666
A kubeconfig copied into a shared
workspace or a CI/CD variable scoped too broadly

11
00:01:04.746 --> 00:01:11.290
can become long-lived cluster access that
is difficult to inventory.

12
00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:15.031
RBAC should match the deployment task.

13
00:01:15.111 --> 00:01:22.986
A script that validates manifests may only
need read or dry-run style capabilities.

14
00:01:23.066 --> 00:01:30.830
A script that applies a release may need
permission for specific resource types in a

15
00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:32.907
specific namespace.

16
00:01:32.987 --> 00:01:40.529
Rollback, secret-management, and
administrative tasks may require different

17
00:01:40.609 --> 00:01:42.051
capabilities.

18
00:01:42.131 --> 00:01:46.457
Separating validate, plan, apply,
rollback,

19
00:01:46.537 --> 00:01:54.523
and administration where practical helps
prevent one script from becoming a general

20
00:01:54.603 --> 00:01:56.933
cluster administrator.

21
00:01:57.013 --> 00:02:00.784
Environment-specific access matters.

22
00:02:00.864 --> 00:02:08.961
A staging deployment identity should not
automatically have production privileges.

23
00:02:09.041 --> 00:02:15.918
A production deployment identity should be
tightly scoped and reviewed.

24
00:02:15.998 --> 00:02:21.988
Avoid broad cluster-admin deployment
identities as a default.

25
00:02:22.068 --> 00:02:24.730
The goal is least privilege:

26
00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:30.245
enough access to perform the intended
deployment safely,

27
00:02:30.325 --> 00:02:36.980
with credentials that can be reviewed,
rotated, and revoked over time.
